task (completed) {
  refactor /guttenberg search <keywords>:
  - use examples like "https://openlibrary.org/search.json?title=the+time+machine&author=h+g+wells", pull the matching Gutenberg text identifier

  Implemented:
  app/chat_server.py
  - `/gutenberg search` now resolves books through the Open Library search
    API. A free-form query such as `the time machine by h g wells` is mapped
    to the documented `?title=...&author=...` form; other queries use `q`.
  - Each matching Open Library work contributes its `id_project_gutenberg`
    value, which becomes the Gutenberg ebook id, page URL, and plaintext URL.
    Results are deduplicated by Gutenberg id and carry the work's title,
    authors, languages, subjects, and first-publish year.
  - Some works (for example the prompt's Time Machine example) are not yet
    linked to a Gutenberg text in Open Library. In that case the Project
    Gutenberg catalogue search page is used as a fallback, parsing ebook ids,
    titles, authors, and download counts. Open Library results always come
    first; the fallback only fills the remaining slots.
  - The plaintext download/cache, `/api/gutenberg_book`, and per-book RAG chat
    are unchanged and work with both sources.
  app/chat.html
  - Book cards show the Open Library first-publish year when available.

  Verified: `pride and prejudice`, `sherlock holmes`, and `frankenstein`
  resolve through Open Library; `the time machine by h g wells` resolves to
  Gutenberg ebook 35 through the fallback, and its plaintext downloads and
  cleans correctly.
}

task (completed) {
  in the left column of the book popup modal, make every single word of the plaintext content of the book dictionary searchable, similar to the behavior the answer  
}

  Implemented:
  app/chat.html
  - The book's plaintext stays as one text node instead of one span per
    word, so multi-megabyte Gutenberg books do not create millions of DOM
    nodes. A pointer-to-caret mapping finds the word under the cursor, moves
    the caret across only that word's characters, and sends a small
    surrounding excerpt to the existing streaming /api/dict popup.
  - Hovering a word shows the same highlight and pointer cursor as answer
    words (using the CSS Custom Highlight API when available). Dragging to
    select text does not start a lookup.
  - Added a "click any word to define" hint to the plaintext pane and
    mentioned Gutenberg text in the keyboard/mouse help.

task (completed) {
  create command /gutenberg {
    - /gutenberg search <keywords>: use https://gutendex.com/books/?search=<keyword>

    - display the result as a list of book links with book title, link to the book (like "https://gutendex.com/books/1342")

    - when click on the book link, open up a modal with 2 columns similar to the youtube video popup {
      column left:  
        - load the plaintext of the book
      column right:
        - ask question on the book, but keep in mind {
          - each book contains large amount of text that consume a lot of context, please find a way to implement a memory efficient, context efficeint and fast RAG for answering question related to this book
        }
    }
  }

  Implemented:
  app/chat_server.py
  - Added /gutenberg search <keywords>. It queries the public Gutendex
    catalog (https://gutendex.com/books/?search=...) and streams a "books"
    SSE event with normalized cards: id, title, authors, languages, subjects,
    download count, the Gutenberg page URL, and the plaintext URL. Searches
    retry once because Gutendex occasionally stalls after accepting a
    connection.
  - Added GET /api/gutenberg_book, which downloads a chosen book's plaintext
    once under .chat/gutenberg_cache/<id>.txt, strips the Project Gutenberg
    header/footer, and also writes a RAG-friendly copy with chapter lines
    promoted to Markdown headings. This endpoint powers the modal's left
    column.
  - Added POST /api/gutenberg_chat. It lazily builds an isolated,
    article-scoped ChatState from the cached plaintext using the existing
    /article section-aware trunk/summary builder, then answers through the
    normal iterative RAG loop. Only the relevant retrieved paragraphs and
    section summaries enter the model context, so novels of millions of
    characters never fill the context window. At most three book sessions are
    kept in memory (LRU), while their downloads and article indexes remain on
    disk. Each book has its own conversation so the main chat is untouched.
  - Fixed Markdown section detection in the shared article structure builder:
    headings are stored without their "##" marker but paragraph text keeps
    it, so Markdown headings (including promoted Gutenberg chapters) now map
    to sections correctly. ARTICLE_META_VERSION was bumped to rebuild old
    caches.

  app/chat.html
  - /gutenberg search renders a two-column book-card grid with title, author,
    language/download/subject metadata, and a "Read & ask" affordance.
  - Clicking a card opens a centered two-column modal. The left pane streams
    in the cached full plaintext. The right pane is a per-book chat backed by
    /api/gutenberg_chat, with streaming answers, dictionary-clickable words,
    Enter/Shift+Enter handling, and Escape/backdrop close.
  - Added /gutenberg subcommand tab completion and a "Project Gutenberg"
    command label.
}
